  (:adr/body
  "# ADR-2607211437: kotoba BIM / Revit 相当統合設計 — IFC 共通化、解析・図面・共同編集の境界と session close

## Status

Accepted for the architecture and merged capabilities listed below. This ADR does **not** declare full Revit parity. It closes the current implementation session and records the remaining gaps and dirty WIP without promoting them to completed work.

## Context

The objective was to establish a serious parametric BIM application in kotoba-lang: parametric families, drawing generation, IFC exchange, structural and MEP design, collaboration, and coordinated publication through cloud-itonami. The highest-priority interoperability gate was external IFC round-trip with a formal spatial hierarchy and geometry mapping, because real exchange must precede deeper authoring features.

The work crossed five repositories. Without an explicit boundary, IFC schema semantics, application authoring state, cloud documents, UI state, and regeneration dependencies would become duplicated authorities.

## Decision

1. **`kotoba-lang/org-iso-16739` owns shared IFC semantics and physical-file interchange.** BIM applications consume it; they do not copy IFC parsers/writers into each app. The reverse-domain name is appropriate because the library implements mappings for ISO 16739 concepts. The name does not itself claim certification or complete conformance.
2. **`kotoba-lang/bim` owns canonical BIM authoring and engineering semantics.** It contains project/spatial hierarchy, parameter and family evaluation, structural models/results, MEP connector networks/results, drawing derivation, regeneration, and semantic worksharing operations.
3. **`kotoba-lang/kami-app-bim-editor` owns interactive presentation and commands, not domain authority.** It binds editor workflows to the canonical BIM and publication contracts.
4. **`gftdcojp/cloud-itonami` owns durable coordination/publication.** External IFC/BCF exchange is represented through OpenCDE-style documents and ordered topics rather than editor-local state.
5. **`kotoba-lang/kotoba-document-contracts` owns reusable artifact dependency vocabulary.** The generic dependency graph is shared; BIM-specific artifact meanings remain in `kotoba-lang/bim`.
6. **Regeneration is dependency-driven and incremental.** Changes rebuild affected structural, MEP, drawing, and IFC artifacts through one coordinated project graph rather than independent ad-hoc refresh paths.
7. **Collaboration conflicts are semantic and auditable.** Resolution operates on BIM entities/properties and preserves resolution history; last-writer-wins is not the sole model.

## Landed capabilities and evidence

- **External IFC round-trip / common library** — `org-iso-16739` PRs #49 and #50 (main merges `8866d0b`, `0e7a630`): spatial/product representation handling, tessellation, mapped origins, faceted and advanced BREP, B-spline knots, CSG primitives, and face-based surface models. Recorded external corpus result: 21/21 files accepted, 139 products, rewrite/semantic/opaque preservation checks, with no newly introduced IfcOpenShell errors.
- **Cloud exchange** — cloud-itonami PR #472 (`274619dd`): durable design sync plus OpenCDE IFC documents and ordered BCF topics.
- **Editor publication/family surface** — editor PRs #61 (`3889237`) and #62 (`7eeba42`): IFC content/checksum/topic publication and family lookup/reporting UI.
- **Parametric family semantics** — BIM PR #117 (`941dea2`): lookup/reporting family behavior.
- **Structural engineering** — BIM PRs #118 (`4d100321`) and #123 (`2a54af8`): canonical 2D integration followed by six-DOF 3D frames, releases, combinations, torsion, biaxial bending, and distributed loads.
- **MEP engineering** — BIM PRs #119 (`cd98ec3`) and #124 (`9b2166c`): authored connector graphs, sizing/fitting losses, and nonlinear closed-loop nodal-pressure solving.
- **Drawings** — BIM PR #120 (`6473de8`): exact mesh/section intersection edges.
- **Coordinated regeneration** — document-contracts PR #3 (`b86e7bee`) plus BIM PR #121 (`7831696`): shared artifact graph and incremental structural/MEP/drawing/IFC regeneration.
- **Worksharing** — BIM PR #122 (`d08a2b9`): semantic conflict resolution with audit history.

All merged PRs above were reviewed and reported green at merge time. Commit identifiers are recorded to make later audits independent of branch names.

## Consequences and non-goals

- IFC support is reusable across kotoba-lang and is no longer coupled to one BIM UI.
- ISO reverse-domain naming means standards ownership/mapping, not an assertion of buildingSMART certification. Formal certification and a wider vendor corpus remain separate acceptance gates.
- Native proprietary RVT/RFA read/write is not promised. The supported interoperability direction is open IFC/BCF/OpenCDE; a proprietary adapter would require a separately licensed and tested decision.
- The structural solver is an engineering-analysis foundation, not yet a full nonlinear finite-element suite. Plate/shell bending, nonlinear materials/geometric effects, design-code checking, meshing, and production validation remain open.
- MEP has connector-authored hydraulic/network analysis, but production electrical distribution, full equipment catalogs, code compliance, and cross-discipline routing/clash workflows remain incomplete.
- Drawing section intersections are exact, but full hidden-line removal, view templates, dimensions/tags/schedules, sheet composition, and production annotation breadth remain gaps.

## Remaining gap order

1. Restore and finish the electrical distribution WIP, then publish it as an isolated reviewed PR.
2. Add plate/shell bending and design-code verification on top of the structural canonical model.
3. Replace remaining approximate drawing visibility with exact hidden-line processing and production documentation primitives.
4. Wire every newer analysis/result surface end-to-end through the editor and cloud workflow.
5. Expand external IFC corpus/performance testing and pursue the required formal conformance/certification process.
6. Run an end-to-end Revit-originated model acceptance scenario: import, edit parameters/families, coordinate structural/MEP/drawings, export, reopen externally, and compare semantics/geometry.

## Session closing / exact resume state

Implementation stops here on 2026-07-21 JST.

`orgs/kotoba-lang/bim` is on local branch `agent/closed-loop-mep-analysis` with four uncommitted electrical-distribution files:

- `src/bim/mep.cljc`
- `src/bim/regeneration.cljc`
- `test/bim/mep_test.cljc`
- `test/bim/regeneration_test.cljc`

The WIP includes three-phase panel balancing, radial downstream load roll-up, feeder/fault/protection analysis, and an `:electrical/distribution` regeneration artifact. It is **not completed**: `clojure -M:test` currently stops while compiling `bim/regeneration.cljc:90` with `No such namespace: mep`. No commit or PR is claimed for this WIP.

The editor checkout also has modified generated files `public/js/app.js` and `public/js/manifest.edn`; ownership was not established in this session, so they were preserved and not included in the BIM WIP claim. The superproject root contains extensive unrelated dirty/untracked state and was likewise left untouched.

Resume by inspecting the four BIM files, resolving the namespace/require boundary, running `clojure -M:test` and `clojure -M:lint`, reviewing the electrical semantics, then committing and publishing only that scoped change. Do not clean, reset, or absorb the unrelated root/editor changes without establishing ownership first.\n\n## 改訂履歴\n\n（2026-07-25 に `90-docs/adr-ledger/adr-ledger.edn` から統合。以後この文書は最新状態のみを表し、履歴は git が持つ。）

### 2026-07-22 — note

Gap-order item 4 advanced further: structural 3D-frame analysis is now wired end-to-end. kotoba-lang/bim PR #126 (merged, cae7338) fixed a real gap generate-structural-model never had test coverage for -- it didn't populate shear-modulus-pa/torsion-m4/inertia-y-m4/inertia-z-m4 on members (added structural-torsion-constant + structural-shear-modulus with reference-value tests) and only produced 3-DOF translational node restraints while analyze-3d-frame-model requires 6-DOF (added six-dof-restraints padding inside analyze-3d-frame-model itself, since analyze-truss still requires restraints length to match point dimension exactly -- validate-structural-model already allowed both 3 and 6 for 3D nodes, confirming this was an intended-but-unimplemented adapter, not a hack). kami-app-bim-editor PR #64 (merged, adds a 'Run six-DOF 3D frame analysis' button reusing structural-result-overlay/the existing deformed-shape render path unchanged (already result-shape-agnostic). bim: 139 tests/918 assertions, 0 lint errors. editor: 27 tests/294 assertions, shadow-cljs 0 warnings. Item 4's only remaining sub-gap is closed-loop authored MEP (bim.integration/analyze-closed-authored-mep-system) wiring into the editor -- current editor MEP routing only uses tree-mode sizing. Items 2 (plate/shell FEA), 3 (exact hidden-line drawings), 5 (IFC corpus/certification), and 6 (Revit-origin acceptance scenario) remain untouched.

### 2026-07-22 — note

Backfill: the prior two progress notes on this ADR (electrical-distribution WIP resumed as bim PR #125, and its editor+cloud wiring as kami-app-bim-editor PR #63) were lost -- they were only ever uncommitted local edits to this ledger file (never git-committed per this repo's opt-in-commit policy) and the working tree copy was reset by an unrelated operation before this note. Restating for the record: bim PR #125 (merged 4e4c622) landed analyze-electrical-distribution; kami-app-bim-editor PR #63 (merged eca51bd) wired it into a radial-distribution UI, an :electrical capability-status key, and generalized OpenCDE publish to carry a media-type per document. Both PRs and their merge commits remain intact on GitHub independent of this ledger note.

### 2026-07-22 — note

Gap-order item 4 complete: all three 'newer analysis surfaces' identified at session close are now wired end-to-end through the editor. kami-app-bim-editor PR #65 (merged b21ba67) adds a closed-loop pipe network UI (four-node diamond topology) calling bim.integration/analyze-closed-authored-mep-system -- verified outside the cljs runtime via clojure -M -e against bim.mep/bim.integration directly: validates clean before/after solving, converges in 16 iterations to ~7e-14 residual, splits demand evenly across symmetric branches. Combined with electrical distribution (bim#125 + editor#63) and structural 3D-frame (bim#126 + editor#64) from earlier this session, item 4 ('wire every newer analysis/result surface end-to-end through the editor and cloud workflow') is done. Remaining ADR-2607211437 gap-order items: 2 (plate/shell FEA + design-code verification), 3 (exact hidden-line drawing removal + production documentation primitives), 5 (external IFC corpus expansion + formal buildingSMART certification process), 6 (end-to-end Revit-originated model acceptance scenario).

### 2026-07-22 — note

Gap-order item 2, design-code-verification half only: kotoba-lang/bim PR #127 (merged 345b832) adds structural-member-utilization -- an elastic combined axial + biaxial bending unity check evaluated at both member ends against factored yield stress, wired into analyze-3d-frame-combination as :structural.analysis/member-checks (previously the six-DOF frame path had zero design-code check, unlike the truss path's axial-only check; structural-result-overlay always rendered frame-3d members in the uncomputed 'no-check' color regardless of real overstress). Explicitly does not cover torsion, shear, lateral-torsional buckling, or second-order/P-delta effects -- not a substitute for a full AISC/Eurocode interaction equation. 140 tests/930 assertions, 0 lint errors; new tests include a direct reference-value check of the helper (governing case, failing case, nil-on-missing-data case) plus end-to-end member-checks + overlay-coloring verification. Plate/shell bending finite elements (the other half of item 2's stated scope) were NOT attempted -- that requires implementing actual shell elements (membrane + bending stiffness, assembly) from scratch, a substantially larger undertaking than this session's remaining budget covers; :structural/shells today are still only used for area-load tributary distribution, not analyzed for their own bending behavior. Items 3 (exact hidden-line drawings), 5 (IFC corpus/certification), 6 (Revit-origin acceptance scenario), and plate/shell FEA within item 2 remain open.

### 2026-07-24 — note

Gap-order items 2/3/6 already substantially done upstream (unrecorded); item 5 confirmed still open; west pin was 212 commits stale

orgs/kotoba-lang/bim の west pin が 2275f22f(212コミット遅れ)で止まっており、GitHub main(7f4f4b8)には未記録の進捗が既に着地していた。pinをfast-forwardで7f4f4b8へ前進(manifest/west.yml、commit 8f58339、単独diff)。判明した実際の状態:\\n\\n item 2(plate/shell bending): 完了。PR #128 が ロッキングフリーのDKT(Discrete Kirchhoff Triangle)plate-bending要素を追加(analyze-plate-bending-mesh)。片持ち梁閉形式収束・単純支持正方形板のTimoshenko&Woinowsky-Krieger係数との比較(0.18%以内)・全体力の釣り合い・Maxwell-Betti相反定理(13桁一致)の4通りで独立検証済み。design-code半分は既にPR #127で完了(structural-member-utilization)。残存gap: 3D flat shell要素(membrane+bending結合、3D埋め込み)は未着手 -- 現状はローカル2D平面のplate定式化のみ。\\n\\n item 3(exact hidden-line + production documentation): 部分完了。PR #129が実多角形重なり判定によるhidden-line occlusionを追加(旧来のAABB厳密部分集合判定を置換、Sutherland-Hodgman clipping)。残存gap: 複数遮蔽物の合成判定は未対応(1つのより近い多角形が単独でカバーする場合のみ判定)、レンダリングは依然bounding-boxで実シルエット輪郭ではない、PDF export(bim.interchange)はocclusion culling自体が無い、dimensions/tags/schedules/sheet構成は未着手。\\n\\n item 5(external IFC corpus拡張 + formal buildingSMART certification process): 未着手を確認。main tip までの219コミット全件のコミットメッセージを検索したが該当作業なし。\\n\\n item 6(Revit-origin acceptance scenario): 完了 + follow-up。PR #130が実在するAutodesk Revit Architecture 2011のIFC2X3 export(test/fixtures/external/revit2011_wall1.ifc、org-iso-16739外部コーパスと共有)を使い、import→property編集→export→再importでのsemantics/geometry比較を実施し2つの実バグを発見(1つは同PRで修正、1つはドキュメント化のみ)。PR #131がCSG half-space meshのクラッシュ2件(2D profile-position/chained half-space clip)を修正。PR #132がIfcRelVoidsElementのopening情報がimport importedElementに伝播しない欠落を修正。残存gap: 角度付き(mitered)cut形状に対するCSG mesh clippingの根本バグは未修正のまま明示的にドキュメント化されている(perpendicular以外の半空間クリップは現状の区間短縮ショートカットでは表現できない、より大きい別課題として温存)。\\n\\nこのADRの実質的な残存gapは: item 2の3D flat shell embedding、item 3の複数遮蔽物合成判定/真のシルエット描画/PDF occlusion/production annotation一式、item 5全体、item 6のangled-cut CSG mesh clippingバグ、の4点に絞られる。

### 2026-07-23 — note

Gap-order item 5, achievable half: kotoba-lang/org-iso-16739 PR #51 (merged 152df8a). Re-verified all 3 existing external test tiers fresh: pinned corpus (21 files/139 products, lossless, matches ADR's stated evidence), external IfcOpenShell schema/WHERE-rule validator (0 errors), and -- new -- the FULL LIVE buildingSMART IDS 1.0 implementer corpus cloned fresh from upstream (not just the pinned local subset), which has grown to 291 cases (from 287 previously assumed) and surfaced 3 real conformance bugs: (1)+(2) property-requirement-matches? returned true unconditionally for existence-only checks, so an empty IFCLABEL or IFCLOGICAL(.U.) incorrectly satisfied 'property must be set' -- fixed by reusing the existing valid-attribute-value? predicate; (3) multiple <xs:pattern> facets in one <xs:restriction> should OR-combine per XSD but the parser overwrote :pattern per element so only the last ever applied -- fixed via an accumulating :patterns collection in parser+matcher+writer. All 3 fixes have network-free regression tests. Also added corpus-runner.clj's first-ever performance budget (previously zero performance testing existed in this repo) -- generous 30s ceiling on the full corpus round-trip, observed baseline ~19s. Post-fix: 291/291 IDS cases pass. Formal buildingSMART certification itself (the ADR's other ask in item 5) is NOT attempted -- it is an external submission/audit process requiring an actual organizational entity, not something achievable through code. Remaining: item 6 (Revit-origin acceptance scenario), which can now legitimately use the corpus's real Revit-exported IFC2X3 fixtures (Revit 2011/2014 wall/half-space exports) as its origin model.

### 2026-07-23 — note

Gap-order item 6, final remaining item: kotoba-lang/bim PR #130 (merged 3350d7a). Real end-to-end acceptance scenario using an unmodified Autodesk Revit Architecture 2011 IFC2X3 wall export (same pinned file already used by org-iso-16739's corpus) -- import, edit a property, coordinate structural + drawings, export, reopen with an independent parse, compare semantics/geometry. Running it surfaced and fixed 2 real crashes: structural-shell-from-element and element-mesh both unconditionally assumed every :kind :wall element has the simple :geometry :axis/:profile shape bim/wall authors, throwing NPEs for imported foreign BREP geometry -- both now gracefully skip/fall-back instead (element-mesh falls back to the already-existing generic geometry-mesh dispatch that already handles :boolean-result, matching the pattern :slab already used). A 3rd, deeper bug was found and explicitly NOT fixed: meshing this wall's specific nested boolean-clipped half-space-solid (its mitered end) throws inside positioned-profile-boundary/boolean-result-mesh -- structural/drawing coordination is instead demonstrated on a companion analytical wall built from the imported wall's own Revit pset dimensions, with the gap documented directly in the test. A 4th, smaller gap also found+documented but not fixed: import-external-ifc drops IfcRelVoidsElement openings entirely. What the scenario DOES verify end-to-end and passing: edited property survives export+reopen, original :geometry survives byte-for-byte, companion wall structural shell + drawing render correctly. 145 tests/958 assertions, 0 lint errors. With this PR, ALL SIX gap-order items from this ADR have real, tested, merged work from this session: items 1 and 4 fully complete; items 2, 3, and 5 substantially complete with explicitly documented remaining sub-scope (plate/shell FEA done but membrane+3D-shell-embedding not; occlusion-decision fixed but rendering still bbox and PDF export uncovered; IDS corpus bugs fixed but formal buildingSMART certification is an external process outside agent scope); item 6 complete with 2 further gaps surfaced and documented rather than silently left unaddressed. Session total across bim/kami-app-bim-editor/org-iso-16739: PRs #63,64,65,125,126,127,128,129,130 (bim/editor) + #51 (org-iso-16739), all merged.

### 2026-07-23 — note

Follow-up to item 6: kotoba-lang/bim PR #131 (merged 5cdc81c) precisely root-causes and partially fixes the CSG mesh crash PR #130 documented but left unfixed. Two real bugs fixed: (1) positioned-profile-boundary destructured a profile's own (frequently 2D, IfcAxis2Placement2D) :position :location into [px py pz] unconditionally, leaving pz nil for 2-element locations -- this was actually the FIRST NPE in the original crash chain, hit during boolean-result-mesh's eager speculative meshing of the unclipped extrusion, fixed by reusing the existing point3 2D/3D-padding helper; (2) clipped-extrusion's fallback only recognized a direct :extruded-area-solid first-operand, never resolving chains of more than one half-space clip (the standard IfcBooleanClippingResult pattern for e.g. a wall end cut by 2 planes) -- added resolve-clipped-extrusion to recurse through arbitrary chain depth. Both independently regression-tested with synthetic geometry. Re-verified against the Revit fixture: element-mesh no longer crashes. Precisely diagnosed remaining gap (NOT fixed): this wall's specific cut is genuinely angled/mitered (~5.7deg off perpendicular to the extrusion axis), and clipped-extrusion's strict near-1.0 dot-product check correctly rejects it since a non-perpendicular half-space cut needs a differently-shaped result (slanted end face) the current interval-shortening shortcut cannot produce -- true fix requires either general angled-plane mesh clipping or building a bounded proxy mesh from a polygonally-bounded half-space's own boundary polygon routed through the existing mesh-csg/mesh-boolean machinery. 147 tests/963 assertions, 0 lint errors. This is a real, incremental improvement over #130's finding, not a full resolution -- the angled-cut gap and the still-unfixed import-external-ifc opening-drop gap from #130 remain open, precisely documented follow-ups.

### 2026-07-24 — note

Second follow-up to item 6: kotoba-lang/bim PR #132 (merged 7f4f4b8) fixes the dropped-openings-on-import gap PR #130 documented but did not fix. Root cause: imported-element only calls attach-imported-openings (axis-relative offset/sill placement via bim/add-opening-to-wall) inside the branch for walls with simple directly-readable geometry -- a complex-geometry wall (e.g. Revit BREP) falls into the generic bim/element fallback that never called it, silently discarding any related openings, even though ifc.core's own parser (verified directly) already correctly resolves IfcRelVoidsElement onto the raw product. Fixed by always attaching raw opening products as a new, deliberately separate :ifc/openings field (not reusing :openings' rectangular-opening offset/sill/width/height shape, which wall-with-openings-mesh/add-opening-to-wall/remove-opening-from-wall depend on -- feeding those an incompatible shape risks silent wrong-answer overlap checks rather than a clean failure). 147 tests/965 assertions, 0 lint errors; acceptance scenario test extended to assert the opening now round-trips with its correct global-id. Remaining open, documented gaps from this session: angled/mitered half-space clipping (partially diagnosed in bim#131, not fixed -- needs general angled-plane mesh clipping or a bounded half-space proxy mesh through mesh-csg), true silhouette rendering vs bounding-box rectangles for drawings, PDF-export occlusion, 3D-shell embedding + membrane coupling for the plate-bending element. Formal buildingSMART certification remains outside agent scope.
")
  ))
